  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   This approach leverages a multi-stage optimization framework that combines sparse initialization with coordinate-wise optimization and adaptive smoothing to tackle the minimization of the maximum shift correlation. The core innovation is the use of a convergent sequence of local updates guided by derivative information and the development of a deep spectral penalty that penalizes worst-case overlaps implicitly. This method addresses the high dimensionality and combinatorial nature of the problem by:\n\n   - Starting with sparse greedy binary representations to establish local optima\n   - Coordinately combining shift feature Manhattan penalization with exponential smoothing \n   - Employing a hierarchy of gradient-based adjustments at varying spectral dampenings\n   - Maintaining dual feasibility enforcement via projection operators\n\n   The method is particularly effective at resolving feature clustering issues and reduces the risk of local minima trapping, which has limited previous computational approaches. It achieves a convergence to lower correlation bounds by progressively relaxing the problem structure through carefully chosen smoothing penalties.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   The problem minimizes:\n   \\[ \\max_k \\left( \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))  dx \\right) \\]\n\n   Subject to:\n   \\begin{aligned}\n      & 0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1 \\quad \\forall x\\\\\n      & \\int_0^2 h(x)  dx = 1\n   \\end{aligned}\n\n   Discretization:\n   \\[ h = (h_i)_{i=1}^{n}, \\quad dx = \\frac{2}{n}, \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^{n} h_i \\cdot dx = 1 \\]\n\n   Formulation:\n   \\begin{align*}\n      \\min_{\\mathbf{h} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n, \\|\\mathbf{h}\\|_n=0.5/dx}  & \\max_k \\frac{2}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{2n-1} |c_k(i)| \\\\\n      \\text{s.t.} \\quad & 0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 \\quad \\forall i \\in \\{1,\\dots,n\\}\n   \\end{align*}\n\n   The convergence procedures employ:\n\n   - Gradient projection with spectral step sizing: $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{h}} \\max_k c_k \\circ \\mathbf{J}$\n   - 1-norm penalization: $\\lambda \\|\\mathbf{h}_{boundary}\\|_1$\n   - 2-norm damping: $\\mu \\|\\nabla^2 \\sum_i h_i\\|_2$\n\n   where $\\mathbf{J}$ is a Jacobian matrix of correlation terms.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   The function `run` follows a sequential optimization strategy:\n\n   a. Initialization (n=400):\n        - Start with provided parent or uniform initialization\n        - Apply depth-clipping and mass concentration\n\n   b. Phase1: Greedy binary proliferation (200 sweeps):\n        - Initialize h from parent using binary projection\n        - Local search for maximum binary placements\n        - Maintain candidate record with improving correlation\n\n   c. Phase2: Spectral coordinate descent (2000 iterations):\n        - Leverage altered differential component (ADC) updates:\n          \\[\\mathbf{h}_{j+1} = \\mathbf{h}_j + \\alpha_j \\text{proj}_{[0,1]}(\\nabla c_{\\text{max}}(\\mathbf{h}_j))\\]\n        - Score update uses:\n          \\[c_{\\text{new}} = \\max_k \\frac{2}{n} |\\text{correlation}_k(\\mathbf{h}_{j+1})|\\]\n\n   d. Phase3: Superposable smoothing with adaptive barrier (3 stages):\n        - Use log-barrier constraints: $ \\mu \\text{KL}(h||\\theta) $\n        - Optimization:  \n          \\[\\min_{\\mathbf{h}} \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{k} c_k(\\mathbf{h}) + \\mu \\|\\mathbf{h} - \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{prev}}\\|_2^2\\]\n        - Solver: CVXPY with ECOS or SCS\n\n   e. Phase4: Final optional coordinate-wise damping (150 steps)\n        - Apply low-rank feature extraction to project current solution\n        - Reconcile with integral constraint\n        - Return Best Candidate recorded during optimization (timescaled by factor based on resolution)\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   Key tunables:\n\n   - Resolution scaling: n \u2208 {400, 600, 800}\n   - Phase parameterization:\n     \u2022 p_factor_adaptive = [0.06, 0.20, 0.55] for n=400\n     \u2022 repeat_backward_passes = ceil(3) based on failure count\n\n   Hyperparameter adjustments:\n\n   ```python\n   core_iterations = {'base': 400, 'sd': 400, 'nls': 300, 'final': 150}\n   correlation_shift_range = {'min': -(n-1), 'max': (n-1), 'mask_step': 3} \n   smoothing_factors = [500, 2000, 10000]\n   projection_multiplier = 0.98\n   ```\n\n   The algorithm includes drift management through periodic projection to feasible set at each SLSQP-bound revision.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   Timings:\n\n   - n=400: ~1.4 * 1100 seconds (wall-time estimate)\n   - n=600: 5.8 * base_time^(n/400)\n   - n=800: degrades exponentially\n\n   Deadline enforcement:\n\n   \\[\\Delta t_{\\text{burst}} = \\min(10, budget_s * 0.1) \\text{ seconds per heavy operation}\\]\n\n   Failure recovery:\n\n   - Minimum acceptable integral: |integral - 0.5| \u2264 1e-5 * 2.0/n * 400\n   - Fallback: revert to candidate with highest confer value\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   Post-optimization requirements:\n\n   - LDPC system: validate integral with tolerance 1e-5\n   - \u03b5-net: correlation integral consistency (five decimals)\n   - Projection: ensure  `h` has exactly n/2 * 2/n * dx \u2248 1 \n\n   Final validation:\n\n   ```python\n   best_candidate = min(candidates, key=lambda x: x['value'])\n   assert abs(best_candidate['integral'] - 0.5) < 1e-5 * 2.0 / best_candidate['n'] * 400\n   for i in range(best_candidate['n']):\n        assert 0 - 1e-5 <= best_candidate['h'][i] <= 1 + 1e-5\n   ```\n\n   Format: (h_values, correlation_bound, n_points)",
